## Error Handling Test Patterns

### 1. Exception Testing
```java
@Test
@DisplayName("Handle [exception] when [scenario]")
void test[Method][Scenario]Handles[Exception]() {
    // Arrange
    [RequestDto] requestObject = create[Entity]Request();
    String errorMessage = "Error occurred";
    APIErrorResponse expectedError = new APIErrorResponse("ERROR", errorMessage, null);
    ResponseEntity<APIErrorResponse> expectedResponseEntity = 
        new ResponseEntity<>(expectedError, HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);
    
    Mockito.when(this.[service].[method](requestObject))
        .thenThrow(new [Exception](errorMessage));
    Mockito.when(this.apiResponder.respondWithError(errorMessage, HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST))
        .thenReturn(expectedResponseEntity);
    
    // Act
    ResponseEntity<?> response = this.[controller].[method](requestObject);
    
    // Assert
    assertThat(response).isEqualTo(expectedResponseEntity);
    Mockito.verify(this.[service]).[method](requestObject);
    Mockito.verify(this.apiResponder).respondWithError(errorMessage, HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);
}
```

## Import Rules

### 1. Factory Import Rules
- **MANDATORY**: Use regular imports for factory classes
- **FORBIDDEN**: Static imports for factory methods and constants
- **REASON**: Follows Spring Boot checkstyle rules and improves code readability

```java
// ✅ CORRECT - Regular import
import io.recruitcrm.microservice.timesheet.testdata.TimesheetTestDataFactory;

// ❌ FORBIDDEN - Static imports
import static io.recruitcrm.microservice.timesheet.testdata.TimesheetTestDataFactory.*;
import static io.recruitcrm.microservice.timesheet.testdata.TimesheetTestDataFactory.Messages;
import static io.recruitcrm.microservice.timesheet.testdata.TimesheetTestDataFactory.createTimesheetRequest;
```

### 2. Usage Pattern
```java
// ✅ CORRECT - Use class name prefix
Integer jobId = TimesheetTestDataFactory.getDefaultJobId();
CreateTimesheetRequestBodyDto request = TimesheetTestDataFactory.createTimesheetRequest();
String message = TimesheetTestDataFactory.Messages.TIMESHEET_CREATED_SUCCESSFULLY;

// ❌ FORBIDDEN - Direct method calls
Integer jobId = getDefaultJobId();
CreateTimesheetRequestBodyDto request = createTimesheetRequest();
String message = Messages.TIMESHEET_CREATED_SUCCESSFULLY;
```

## Best Practices

### 1. Test Organization
- Group related tests in the same test class
- Use descriptive test names and display names
- Follow the AAA pattern (Arrange, Act, Assert)
- Keep tests independent and isolated
- **Use factory methods for all test data creation**
- **AVOID static imports for factory methods and constants**

### 2. Mock Usage
- Mock all external dependencies
- Use `@Mock` for pure unit testing (recommended)
- Verify important mock interactions
- Don't over-verify trivial interactions
- **Use factory constants for success messages**

### 3. Test Data
- **Use factory methods instead of manual test data creation**
- Create helper methods for complex test data
- Use constants for repeated values
- Keep test data minimal but sufficient
- **Avoid hardcoded values in test methods**

### 4. Assertions
- Use AssertJ for fluent assertions
- Test both positive and negative scenarios
- Verify response structure and content
- Check HTTP status codes
- **Use factory methods for expected responses**

### 5. Coverage
- **PRIORITY**: Achieve 100% code coverage with minimum test cases
- Test all public controller methods
- Test only existing branches/paths in the code
- Test only existing error handling paths
- **AVOID**: Assumption-based edge cases that don't exist in controller logic
- **AVOID**: Testing service layer logic (that's service layer's responsibility)
- **AVOID**: Testing framework behavior (Spring validation, etc.)

## Common Patterns Summary

### 1. Standard Test Structure with Factory
```java
@Test
@DisplayName("Clear description of what is being tested")
void testMethodNameScenarioExpectedResult() {
    // Arrange - Use factory methods for all test data
    Integer entityId = getDefault[Entity]Id();
    RequestDto request = create[Entity]Request();
    ResponseDto expectedResponse = create[Entity]Response();
    ResponseEntity<APINormalResponse<ResponseDto>> expectedEntity = 
        create[Entity]SuccessResponse(expectedResponse);
    
    // Mock setup
    Mockito.when(this.service.method(entityId, request)).thenReturn(expectedResponse);
    Mockito.when(this.apiResponder.respond(expectedResponse, Messages.SUCCESS_MESSAGE))
        .thenReturn(expectedEntity);
    
    // Act - Execute the method under test
    ResponseEntity<?> response = this.controller.method(entityId, request);
    
    // Assert - Verify results and interactions
    assertThat(response).isEqualTo(expectedEntity);
    Mockito.verify(this.service).method(entityId, request);
    Mockito.verify(this.apiResponder).respond(expectedResponse, Messages.SUCCESS_MESSAGE);
}
```

### 2. Mock Verification Pattern
```java
// Verify service interaction
Mockito.verify(this.[service]).[method](expectedParameters);

// Verify API responder interaction with factory constants
Mockito.verify(this.apiResponder).respond(expectedData, Messages.SUCCESS_MESSAGE);
```

### 3. Response Assertion Pattern
```java
// Verify complete response using factory methods
assertThat(response).isEqualTo(expectedResponseEntity);

// Additional specific assertions if needed
assertThat(response.getStatusCode()).isEqualTo(HttpStatus.OK);
```

### 4. Factory Import Pattern
```java
// Import factory class (NOT static imports)
import io.recruitcrm.microservice.timesheet.testdata.[Entity]TestDataFactory;
```

## Complete Test Class Example

```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class TimesheetControllerTests {

    @Mock
    private TimesheetService timesheetService;

    @Mock
    private APIResponder apiResponder;

    @InjectMocks
    private TimesheetController timesheetController;

    @BeforeEach
    void setUp() {
        // @InjectMocks handles dependency injection automatically
    }

    @Test
    @DisplayName("Create timesheet successfully")
    void testCreateTimesheetValidRequestCreatesTimesheet() {
        // Arrange - Use factory methods
        Integer jobId = getDefaultJobId();
        CreateTimesheetRequestBodyDto request = createTimesheetRequest();
        TimesheetResponseBodyDto expectedResponse = createTimesheetResponse();
        ResponseEntity<APINormalResponse<TimesheetResponseDto>> expectedEntity = 
            createTimesheetSuccessResponse(expectedResponse);
        
        // Mock setup
        Mockito.when(this.timesheetService.createTimesheet(jobId, request))
            .thenReturn(expectedResponse);
        Mockito.when(this.apiResponder.respond(expectedResponse, Messages.TIMESHEET_CREATED_SUCCESSFULLY))
            .thenReturn(expectedEntity);
        
        // Act
        ResponseEntity<?> response = this.timesheetController.createTimesheet(jobId, request);
        
        // Assert
        assertThat(response).isEqualTo(expectedEntity);
        Mockito.verify(this.timesheetService).createTimesheet(jobId, request);
        Mockito.verify(this.apiResponder).respond(expectedResponse, Messages.TIMESHEET_CREATED_SUCCESSFULLY);
    }
}
```

These rules ensure consistent, maintainable, and comprehensive controller unit tests across the timesheet microservice using test data factories.
